import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The auth package, a plugin: the package's example app file by file, how signing in goes (a mailed
## link, a provider, the dev sign-in), who gets in, sessions and the middleware, roles, what a user
## may do, two-factor sign-in, the providers, what a failed sign-in says, the tables, safety, and
## what comes next. Static: signing in needs a server.
meta = {
	title: "Auth",
	description: "Sign-in for Roc Solid apps, as a plugin: by a mailed link or at Google, GitHub and any OpenID Connect provider; two-factor sign-in with an authenticator app; sessions kept as hashed tokens in the app's database; middleware that hands the signed-in user on. No pages: the app's own.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "example", label: "A first app" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How signing in goes" },
		{ id: "admit", label: "Who gets in" },
		{ id: "users", label: "Users" },
		{ id: "sessions", label: "Sessions and middleware" },
		{ id: "impersonation", label: "Impersonation" },
		{ id: "roles", label: "Roles" },
		{ id: "permissions", label: "What a user may do" },
		{ id: "two-factor", label: "Two-factor sign-in" },
		{ id: "providers", label: "Providers" },
		{ id: "failed", label: "When it fails" },
		{ id: "tables", label: "The tables" },
		{ id: "safety", label: "Safety" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/auth</code>]}
				title="Sign-in you don't write yourself"
			>
				People sign in by a mailed link or at Google, GitHub or any OpenID Connect provider, plus a code
				from an authenticator app once they turn that on. Sessions live in your app's database, and
				middleware hands the signed-in user to your routes.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				The package is a plugin that does the parts easy to get wrong: tokens kept in plain text, links
				that work twice, redirects to another site, OAuth without its state or PKCE. Your app keeps three:
				the pages, the email the link goes out in, and <code>admit!</code>, which says who gets in and which
				of your users they are.
			</p>

			<Heading id="example">A first app</Heading>
			<p>
				Each file below is from <code>packages/auth/example</code>, a small app whose tests sign in every
				way, on SQLite and on Postgres, against a stand-in provider.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>1. The plugin and its routes.</strong> The app's header names the package; its plugin lists
				the providers offered. The email plugin sends the links (under <code>--dev</code> with no SMTP
				server, they land in the <a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_email}#mailbox"}>dev mailbox</a>).
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_main.file} source={Sources.auth_example_main.rocx} />
			<p>
				Each step of signing in is your route under <code>routes/auth/</code>, calling one of the package's
				handlers. Its folder's middleware must let a signed-out request through. Add the steps you use, and
				pass <code>admit!</code> to the ones that let someone in:
			</p>
			<Code title="routes/auth/" source={"# [provider]/+server.roc: GET /auth/<provider>?then=…, off to a provider\nrespond! = |{ request, params, .. }| Auth.provider_route!(request, Accounts.providers, params.provider)\n# [provider]/callback/+server.roc: back from it (the redirect URI to register)\nrespond! = |{ request, params, .. }| Auth.callback_route!(request, Accounts.providers, params.provider, Accounts.admit!)\n# link/+server.roc: a mailed link\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| Auth.link_route!(request, Accounts.admit!)\n# code/+server.roc: a sign-in's second factor\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| Auth.code_route!(request)\n# sign-out/+server.roc\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| Auth.sign_out_route!(request)\n# dev/+server.roc: any address, under --dev only\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| Auth.dev_route!(request, Accounts.admit!)"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<strong>2. Who gets in</strong> (<a href="#admit"><code>admit!</code></a>). Here anyone gets in but
				addresses at <code>refused.example</code>, and <a href="#users"><code>Auth.user_for!</code></a> finds
				their user, or makes one:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_accounts.file} source={Sources.auth_example_accounts.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>3. The middleware.</strong> <code>Auth.required</code> guards routes for signed-in people,
				<code>Auth.optional</code> routes for anyone. Each takes a function that puts the session into the
				context your routes read:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_middlewares.file} source={Sources.auth_example_middlewares.rocx} />
			<p>
				A directory's <code>+middleware.roc</code> wraps the routes in it. The example's signed-in routes sit
				in a group, <code>routes/(signed-in)/</code>, which adds nothing to their paths; the sign-in pages
				sit outside it:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_signed_in.file} source={Sources.auth_example_signed_in.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<strong>4. The sign-in page's endpoint</strong>, under <code>api/</code> at the page's path
				(<code>/api/sign-in</code>). <code>Auth.link!</code> keeps a link and gives you its URL to mail as
				you like. Its <code>GET</code>, which the page reads, lists the providers set up, for their buttons:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_sign_in.file} source={Sources.auth_example_sign_in.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>5. The page a link opens.</strong> With <code>link_page</code>, a mailed link opens your page,
				which shows whom it signs in and posts the link to <code>/auth/link</code> from a button. Some mail
				services open every link to check it, so a link that signed in on opening would be used up before
				its person clicked it.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_link.file} source={Sources.auth_example_link.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_link_page.file} source={Sources.auth_example_link_page.rocx} />

			<Heading id="how">How signing in goes</Heading>
			<p>
				<strong>By a mailed link.</strong> <code>{"Auth.link!(email, then)"}</code> stores the link (its
				token's hash, the address, where it goes after, its expiry) and gives you the URL. A link works
				once, for 15 minutes (<code>link_ms</code>). Your page posts its token to <code>/auth/link</code>;
				without a <code>link_page</code>, the URL is <code>/auth/link</code> itself.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>At a provider.</strong> Your button links to <code>{"/auth/<provider>?then=…"}</code>. The
				plugin keeps a state, a PKCE verifier and a nonce in a cookie for ten minutes and sends the browser
				to the provider. Back at <code>{"/auth/<provider>/callback"}</code>, the state must match this
				browser's. The code is traded for who signed in: an ID token (OpenID Connect), or GitHub's API.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Under <code>--dev</code>.</strong> <code>POST /auth/dev</code> with any <code>email</code> signs
				it in at once, with no mail. Without <code>--dev</code> the route answers 404. It is for trying the
				app and for its tests: never start a server others can reach with <code>--dev</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Your own way.</strong> <code>{"Auth.sign_in!(answer, user)"}</code> starts a session for a user
				you chose yourself (a password you checked, a person picked from a list) and sets its cookie. An app
				may sign people in this way alone, with no link or provider routes.
			</p>
			<p>
				The other ways end alike: <code>admit!</code> is asked, a session starts, and the browser goes on,
				with the session cookie, to where it was going. That is always a path on your site: a link or a
				provider can't send anyone to another site.
			</p>

			<Heading id="admit">Who gets in</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>admit!</code> is given who is signing in, an <code>Auth.Identity</code>:

			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>provider : Str</code></td><td><code>"email"</code> for a link or the dev sign-in, else the provider's name</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>subject : Str</code></td><td>who they are there: the address, or the provider's own id for them</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>email : Str</code></td><td>their address, lower-cased; <code>""</code> when the provider has none it says is verified</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>name : Str</code></td><td>their name, or <code>""</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>user : [New, Known(Str)]</code></td><td>the user this identity signed in as before, even if its address has changed since</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>then : Str</code></td><td>where the sign-in goes after: an invite's page, say, which <code>admit!</code> may act on</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				It answers <code>Ok(user)</code>, your id for their user, or <code>Err(why)</code>, which the sign-in
				page shows. It runs inside the transaction that starts the session, so its writes commit with the
				session (a new user's row, a welcome email queued as a job) or with its refusal (a request to join a
				waiting list). A database error rolls it all back. Keep slow work, such as a request to another
				server, out of it.
			</p>

			<Heading id="users">Users</Heading>
			<p>
				The package keeps your users: <code>{"Auth.user_for!(identity)"}</code>, called from
				<code>admit!</code>, gives the id of the user an identity is, and makes one if it is no one yet. An
				identity that signed in before is its user, and when the provider gives another address, the user's
				changes with it (unless another user has that address). A new identity is the user with its
				address, so a mailed link and Google with the same verified address are one person, or else a new
				user, whose id is a <a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_crypto}#modules"}>version 7 UUID</a>. A new
				identity with no verified address is <code>NoAddress</code>, for you to refuse. Each user is a row
				of <code>auth_users</code>: the id, the address as it is now, and when they were made. Someone
				you name before they sign in (an invitation to their address, an admin letting them in ahead) is
				<code>{"Auth.user_with_address!(email)"}</code>, made if there is none; their first sign-in finds them.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="the package's users"
				source={"Auth.user_for! : Auth.Identity => Try(Str, [NoAddress, DbErr(Str), BadRow(Str), ..])\nAuth.user! : Str => Try(Auth.User, [NotFound, DbErr(Str), BadRow(Str), ..])            # by id\nAuth.user_by_email! : Str => Try(Auth.User, [NotFound, DbErr(Str), BadRow(Str), ..])   # by address\nAuth.user_with_address! : Str => Try(Str, [NotAnAddress, DbErr(Str), BadRow(Str), ..]) # made if none\nAuth.forget! : Str => Try({}, [DbErr(Str), ..])                                          # the user is gone\n\nAuth.User : { id : Str, email : Str, created_ms : I64 }"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				Keep what else you know of a person in your own tables, by that id: a profile, their
				organizations, what they made. Your queries may join <code>auth_users</code> for an address; only
				the package writes it. Their address stays out of your rows, so it changes in one place, and
				deleting a person is <code>{"Auth.forget!(id)"}</code> and your own rows: it ends their sessions
				(an admin acting as them is themselves again) and deletes their identities, links on their way,
				authenticator, recovery codes, grants and row, all at once. Signing in again later makes a new user.
			</p>

			<Heading id="sessions">Sessions and middleware</Heading>
			<p>
				A session is a random token in a cookie, and a database row that keeps only its SHA-256. It lasts
				30 days (<code>session_ms</code>). Used in its second half, it is extended by as long again, so
				requests don't write to the database each time.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>Auth.required</code> lets a signed-in request through. A signed-out page goes to your sign-in
					page and comes back after (<code>?then=</code>); anything else gets a 401 with the plugin's
					<code>refusal</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Auth.required_with</code> also reads your user's row: a session whose user is gone ends and
					answers as signed out. The example's <code>Middlewares.roc</code> uses it.
				</li>
				<li><code>Auth.optional</code> hands on <code>SignedIn(session)</code> or <code>SignedOut</code>.</li>
				<li>
					<code>Auth.sessions!</code> lists a user's sessions, <code>Auth.revoke!</code> ends one, and
					<code>Auth.sign_out_everywhere!</code> ends them all.
				</li>
				<li>A form that posts to <code>/auth/sign-out</code> signs out.</li>
				<li>
					Your own way in: <code>Auth.sign_in!</code> (above),
					<code>{"Auth.switch!(request, answer, user)"}</code> when someone picks another person, and
					<code>{"Auth.path!(then)"}</code> for where they may go after.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="impersonation">Impersonation</Heading>
			<p>
				An admin helping someone can see the app as they do. <code>{"Auth.impersonate!(request, user, ms)"}</code>
				makes the admin's session act as that user for a while: every route sees them.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					The session stays the admin's (<code>session.real_user</code>). It goes back when its time runs
					out or on <code>Auth.stop_impersonating!</code>, and each start and stop is logged with both users.
				</li>
				<li>You decide who may impersonate before calling it; the example allows the addresses in <code>ADMIN_EMAILS</code>.</li>
				<li>Use <code>real_user</code> to record who did what, and to guard what only a person may change themselves.</li>
				<li>No two-factor code is asked of an admin acting as someone: nobody signs in.</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="roles">Roles</Heading>
			<p>
				What a user holds, and why: Pro because they pay, because they are on a trial, or because an admin
				gave it. A role is one of your own tags, and so is the reason, so a misspelt one doesn't compile.
				<code>Roles</code> is a module of the package (<code>import auth.Roles</code>); its table comes with
				the plugin.
			</p>
			<Code title="Roles" source={"Role : [Pro, Admin]           # your own tags\nVia : [Paid, Trial, Gift]     # why someone holds one\n\nRoles.grant!({ user, role: Pro, via: Gift, by: admin })                          # for good\nRoles.grant!({ user, role: Pro, via: Paid, by: \"stripe\", ends: At(period_end) })\nRoles.revoke!({ user, role: Pro, via: Gift, by: admin })\nRoles.has!(user, Pro)                                                            # Bool\nRoles.current!(user)                                                             # [Pro]: held now\nRoles.of!(user)                                                                  # every grant, past ones too\nRoles.forget!(user)                                                              # the user is gone"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					Someone holds a role while any of their grants of it lasts, one grant for each reason: a
					subscription that ends never takes away a gift, and a gift isn't cut short by a payment.
				</li>
				<li>
					Granting again while a grant lasts moves its end, so a payment that grants until the next period's
					end each month stays one grant. Two grants at once give one.
				</li>
				<li>
					Nothing is deleted but by <code>forget!</code>: grants that ran out or were revoked stay, so
					<code>of!</code> can show what ended when and who ended it, and you can tell someone had a trial
					already (the example's <code>/trial</code> does).
				</li>
				<li>
					You decide who may grant before calling it, as with impersonation; each grant and revoke is logged.
					<code>has!</code> says no when the database can't be read, so what it guards stays shut.
				</li>
				<li>
					Paid roles come from your payments: with <a href={Paths.ecosystem_stripe}>Stripe</a>, a function that
					reads the user's subscription and grants Pro until its period's end, or revokes it once it ended, run
					for every event about them. It reads the state, not the event, so events that come twice or out of
					order change nothing.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="permissions">What a user may do</Heading>
			<p>
				Roles are stored; what a user may do is not. It is a function of your own over what the request
				knows: the roles its middleware read once with <code>Roles.current!</code>, and anything else (a
				list of admins, the org they act in). A route asks for an action, not the roles behind it
				(<code>Policy.need(context, UsePro)?</code>), so a rule changes in one place, and
				<code>expect</code> tests it. The example's, beside its middleware's <code>Me</code> (above):
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_policy.file} source={Sources.auth_example_policy.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					It takes an open record, <code>{"{ roles, admin, .. }"}</code>: a request's context is one, and so is a
					record a job builds for someone (their roles from <code>current!</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					Each action can say its own refusal: a 402 that offers Pro for one, a 404 for an admin page.
				</li>
				<li>
					A check about a thing is an action that carries it, <code>Edit(post)</code>: the route reads the
					post, then asks.
				</li>
				<li>
					A folder of routes asks once, in its <code>+middleware.roc</code>. Behind impersonation, the roles
					are the user acted as, and <code>admin</code> is who really signed in.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="two-factor">Two-factor sign-in</Heading>
			<p>
				With <code>two_factor: Bool.True</code> in the plugin's options, users may turn on two-factor
				sign-in. Then, after their link or provider, they enter the six-digit code their authenticator app
				shows (1Password, Google Authenticator, a phone's passwords app), or a recovery code. The codes are
				TOTP (RFC 6238), which every authenticator app makes.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Turning it on.</strong> <code>{"Auth.two_factor_start!(user, account)"}</code> makes a secret and
				gives you its <code>otpauth://</code> URI, to show as a QR code (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_qr}>packages/qr</a>'s
				<code>QrCode</code>), and the secret as text for an app that can't scan. The first code the app
				shows turns it on and gives ten recovery codes, each good once in place of a code. Show them once:
				only their hashes are kept. The example's endpoints:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_two_factor.file} source={Sources.auth_example_two_factor.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_two_factor_confirm.file} source={Sources.auth_example_two_factor_confirm.rocx} />
			<p>
				<code>Auth.two_factor_disable!</code> turns it off and <code>Auth.two_factor_regenerate!</code> makes new
				recovery codes, each given a code the app shows now, or a recovery code.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Signing in.</strong> For a user with it on, the link or provider doesn't sign in yet: the
				sign-in waits ten minutes for their code, and the browser goes to your code page
				(<code>code_page</code>, <code>/sign-in/code</code>). Meanwhile your middleware treats them as signed
				out, but sends a page to the code page, not the sign-in page. The code page posts the code to
				<code>/auth/code</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_code_page.file} source={Sources.auth_example_code_page.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					A code is good now or for 30 seconds either side (a phone's clock may be a little out), and works
					once: a used code is refused, even in time.
				</li>
				<li>A recovery code works once, and its use is logged.</li>
				<li>
					A right code starts the session under a new token, so the token the browser waited with signs no
					one in. A wrong one comes back with <code>?error=code</code>; the fifth ends the sign-in.
				</li>
				<li>
					A user's wrong codes also count across sign-ins, turning it off and making new recovery codes:
					after ten in a row, their codes are refused for 15 minutes (<code>TooManyTries</code>,
					<code>?error=locked</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<strong>Who must have it</strong> is yours to decide: <code>{"Auth.two_factor!(user)"}</code> says
				whether it is on, and a route that requires it refuses those without. The example's admins must
				have it:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.auth_example_admin.file} source={Sources.auth_example_admin.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>The secret at rest</strong> is sealed with the platform's <code>AesGcm</code>, under a key derived
				from <code>APP_SECRET</code> (32 characters or more: <code>openssl rand -hex 32</code>). With
				two-factor on, the server won't start without it, except under <code>--dev</code>, where a
				development secret stands in. Don't change it: every authenticator's codes would stop working, and
				only recovery codes would sign in.
			</p>
			<Note>
				The app <code>rocx new</code> makes has all of it: an account-page section that sets it up with a QR
				code and turns it on and off, and a code page.
			</Note>

			<Heading id="providers">Providers</Heading>
			<p>
				Register an app with the provider using the redirect URI below, after the server's public URL
				(<code>PUBLIC_URL</code>), and give the server its client id and secret. A provider without a client
				id isn't offered: <code>Auth.providers!</code> leaves its button out.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Needs</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>Auth.google</code></td>
							<td><code>GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET</code>; redirect URI <code>/auth/google/callback</code></td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>Auth.github</code></td>
							<td><code>GITHUB_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET</code>; callback URL <code>/auth/github/callback</code></td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>{"Auth.oidc({ name, label, issuer, authorize_url, token_url, client_id_env, client_secret_env })"}</code></td>
							<td>any OpenID Connect provider; redirect URI <code>{"/auth/<name>/callback"}</code></td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A record update changes any part of one: <code>{"{ ..Auth.google, client_id_env: \"ACME_GOOGLE_ID\" }"}</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="failed">When it fails</Heading>
			<p>
				A failed sign-in goes to your sign-in page with <code>?error=</code> and where it was going
				(<code>{"&then="}</code>):
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th><code>error</code></th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>link</code></td><td>the link has been used or has expired (an expired one still says where it was going)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>refused</code></td><td><code>admit!</code> said no; <code>{"&reason="}</code> is what it said, <code>{"&email="}</code> the address it turned away</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>provider</code></td><td>the provider refused, couldn't be reached, or isn't set up; <code>{"&reason="}</code> says which</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>state</code></td><td>back from a provider too late, or in another browser</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>unavailable</code></td><td>the database couldn't take it just then</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>attempts</code></td><td>the fifth wrong code ended a sign-in waiting for its code</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>locked</code></td><td>the user had given ten wrong codes in a row: their codes are refused for a quarter of an hour after the last</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>timeout</code></td><td>no sign-in was waiting for a code in this browser: ten minutes went by</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>A wrong code goes back to your code page with <code>?error=code</code>.</p>
			<p>
				<code>auth.SignInError</code> words them: <code>SignInError.message(query)</code> gives the sentence for
				a sign-in page's query, and <code>SignInError.field(query, "then")</code> reads a field. It names no
				platform, so it works in the browser too.
			</p>

			<Heading id="tables">The tables</Heading>
			<p>
				The plugin's migrations make its tables, the same on SQLite and Postgres:
				<code>auth_sessions</code>, <code>auth_links</code> (links on their way),
				<code>auth_identities</code> (which of your users each identity signed in as), and for two-factor
				<code>auth_two_factor</code> (sealed secrets), <code>auth_recovery_codes</code> (their hashes) and
				<code>auth_waiting</code> (sign-ins waiting for a code), <code>auth_roles</code> (every grant,
				past ones too), and <code>auth_users</code> (<a href="#users">your users</a>: an id, an address,
				when they were made). Everything else about a person is yours, in your tables, by their id.
			</p>

			<Heading id="safety">Safety</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>Tokens are 32 random bytes, kept only as their SHA-256: a leaked table signs no one in.</li>
				<li>A link is used and deleted in one statement, so two clicks can't both sign in.</li>
				<li>
					Cookies are <code>HttpOnly</code> and <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, and on HTTPS <code>Secure</code>
					and <code>__Host-</code>, so no other site under your domain can set them.
				</li>
				<li>
					OAuth's state is compared in constant time, PKCE binds the code to the browser that asked, and the
					nonce binds the ID token to it. The ID token's issuer, audience and expiry are checked, and an
					address counts only if the provider says it is verified.
				</li>
				<li>Links in mail use the server's public URL, never a request's <code>Host</code>.</li>
				<li>
					An authenticator's secret is sealed (AES-256-GCM) and never kept in clear; recovery codes are 80
					random bits, kept as their SHA-256.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Note>
				A form on another site can't post to yours: the platform refuses it
				(<a href={"${Paths.safety}#origin"}>why</a>). Put a <a href={Paths.ecosystem_guard}>rate limit</a>
				on your sign-in endpoint, per address and per client (its directory's <code>+middleware.roc</code>),
				so nobody can mail an address a hundred links. The app <code>rocx new</code> makes has it.
			</Note>

			<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
			<p>
				Everything above is built and tested. Passwords come next: they need a slow hash (Argon2) that the
				server's host will compute. Passkeys come later: they need the host to verify a P-256 signature,
				which it can make but not yet check. Both are in the package's <code>PROPOSALS.md</code>.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
